  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What does the diaphragm do?",
      "a": "The diaphragm is a dome of muscle under your lungs. When it contracts it flattens and moves down, enlarging the chest so air is drawn in. When it relaxes it rises and air flows out.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the diaphragm",
        "Which muscle makes us breathe?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does air rush into the lungs?",
      "a": "When the chest gets bigger, the air inside the lungs spreads out and its pressure drops below the air pressure outside. Air always flows from higher to lower pressure, so it rushes in.",
      "asks": [
        "How does breathing in work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do lungs have muscles?",
      "a": "Lungs have no muscles to inflate themselves. They are like stretchy bags pulled open by the diaphragm and rib muscles, and they spring back when those muscles relax.",
      "asks": [
        "Can lungs move by themselves?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What do the rib muscles do in breathing?",
      "a": "Muscles between the ribs, called intercostals, lift the ribcage up and out when you breathe in, making the chest wider. They work harder when you breathe deeply.",
      "asks": [
        "Tell me about the rib muscles"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is breathing out work for the body?",
      "a": "At rest, breathing out is mostly passive: the muscles relax and the stretched lungs recoil. When you blow hard or exercise, belly and rib muscles push the air out faster.",
      "asks": [
        "Do you need muscles to breathe out?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "The diaphragm is the main muscle of quiet breathing.",
    "At rest, breathing out is mainly passive, driven by the elastic recoil of the lungs."
  ],
